"The first biography of this great and tragic poet that takes advantage of a wealth of new material, this is an unusually balanced, comprehensive and definitive life of Sylvia Plath. Determined not to read Plath's work as if her every act, from childhood on, was a harbinger of her tragic fate, Clark presents new materials about Plath's scientist father, her juvenile writings, and her psychiatric treatment, and evokes a culture in transition in the mid-twentieth century, in the shadow of the atom bomb and the Holocaust, as she explores Sylvia's world: her early relationships and determination not to become a conventional woman and wife; her conflicted ties to her well-meaning, widowed mother; her troubles at the hands of an unenlightened mental-health industry; and her Cambridge years and thunderclap meeting with Ted Hughes, a true marriage of minds that would change the course of poetry…","readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"crossover","narrativeScore":0.293,"accessibilityScore":0.176,"scholarlyScore":0,"traits":[{"id":"biographical","score":1,"confidence":"high","evidence":["catalog text contains \"life of\"","catalog text contains \"biography of\"","catalog text contains \"father\"","catalog text contains \"mother\"","biography subject or topic","biography award category"]},{"id":"character_driven","score":0.74,"confidence":"high","evidence":["catalog text contains \"life of\"","catalog text contains \"biography of\"","catalog text contains \"father\"","catalog text contains \"mother\""]},{"id":"essayistic","score":0.38,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"criticism\"","criticism award category"]},{"id":"popular","score":0.34,"confidence":"low","evidence":["trade publisher signal: Jonathan Cape / Penguin Random House","major-award recognition"]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"renownProfile":{"knowsBook":true,"publicFame":67,"criticalRenown":84,"controversy":24,"llmAffinity":86,"affinityResidual":2.41,"confidence":{"publicFame":0.98